Ulrich Plate <plate@gSPAM_ME_NEVERol.com> writes:
> Apologies for deviating from this thread's Windows orientation, I'm using
> 3.4.3 on a Linux host, compiled from sources. I may be particularly thick
> today, but wizard mode appears to be commented out in include/config.h,
> like so:
> 
> #ifndef WIZARD          /* allow for compile-time or Makefile changes */
> # ifndef KR1ED
> #  define WIZARD  "wizard" /* the person allowed to use the -D option */
> # else
> #  define WIZARD
> #  define WIZARD_NAME "wizard"
> # endif
> #endif
> 
> I've tried uncommenting just one (define WIZARD "username"), then some,
> finally all of these lines, but I'm getting syntax errors from every
> single permutation.

You're probably confusing the C preprocessor directive symbol ('#')
with shell- or perl-style comment denoters; C comments are delimited by
'/* */' pairs. Removing the # before a define will break it :-)

> Since my knowledge of C never really made it past
> printf("hello world\n"); could someone please tell me which lines to put
> into include/config.h so that wizard mode works for my user account?

Just replace "wizard" with "username" in the line commented
"/* the person allowed to use the -D option */", and leave the rest
alone. (You're not going to be using pre-ANSI C, so you can ignore the
WIZARD_NAME part.)

